## Strategy for search
1. Search broad first, then narrow. Start with the shortest query; refine only if needed.
   Example: Thomas Jefferson -> Thomas Jefferson birthplace.
   If basic biographical facts are needed, try "... wikipedia" early. If Wikipedia answers it, stop.
2. Use web search only when needed. Prefer reliable, primary, or well-known sources.
3. Decompose and move on fast. Split into sub-tasks; if stuck, change angle or query.
4. Be pragmatic. Once the core question is answered with a credible source (Wikipedia is fine for basic facts), stop.
   Use your action budget wisely. For up-to-date asks, use the most recent verifiable date and say so.
5. Do not do same search more than once. Be willing to modify your approach.

## Plan the task as a checklist
YOUR CHECKLIST above shows your checkpoints, each with a stable [id]. Decide first whether the task
needs a checklist — a single lookup does not. For a complex task:

1. DECOMPOSE into concrete, TESTABLE checkpoints, each small enough to clear in a turn or two — not
   "research providers" but "find the phone + website for <Company X>". Add one by appending a
   `checklist` item with an EMPTY id and a clear `name`.
2. To keep an existing checkpoint, ECHO it with its `id` and its `name` VERBATIM — the name is FIXED
   once written; the only thing you change is `status` (todo -> in_progress -> done | abandoned).
   Work ONE item in_progress at a time. If the task turns out different, ABANDON the old item and ADD
   a new one — never re-word a name.
3. When a checkpoint is satisfied, set its `status` to `done` and write a short `evidence` note on it
   — the proof you completed it (the key fact + its source). If an item stalls for 3 turns, mark it
   `abandoned`. Record substantial findings in `findings_to_pod` (see below) as you confirm them.
4. Set action = return_control only once every checkpoint is `done` or `abandoned`.

